Thank you very much Quresh, this is very helpful, and I agree it is more a matter of interpretation than of bias. Your point about overlap is exactly what I am now trying to think through. Of course, all the species we study have home ranges far larger than a camera's field of view, so in a sense we are never really sampling "the site", only a tiny, imperfect window into it. I think a trail may be channeling movement from a wider area than a random point would. If so, the effective area represented by that camera changes, and it may differ between the random and the preferential camera at the same site. It would also not be easy to guarantee consistency across different sites in terms of trails.

This is the part I still need to work out. One idea I am considering is to treat the buffer around the random point as the primary unit (site), and the two cameras (random and trail) as secondary units nested within it, as in Nichols et al. 2008. Site-level occupancy (psi) would then capture whether the species uses the broader area, availability probability (theta) would capture whether it is available at that particular sub-unit, with placement type as a covariate there, and detection probability (p) would stay as is. This way, the effect of placing a camera on a trail would be hopefully absorbed into theta rather than into psi, which is the quantity we actually care about.
Thanks again,

Simone

El viernes, 30 de octubre de 2026 a las 17:57:31 UTC+1, quresh...@birdconservancy.org escribió:
> My two cents - you can think about this in terms of bias, or you can think about it in terms of whether and how available inference from your occupancy estimates change under different designs. I think putting the camera traps along trails as you describe it sounds similar to timing breeding bird surveys during peak singing in the morning. The catch is that you need to keep in mind if and how the overlap between the sampled area and space use / territories might change under alternative designs. I'd have to think about your other questions more to offer useful responses. Instead, I'll offer a couple papers that might help you (and maybe your team) think through your questions.
