Hi Simone,

Really interesting discussion you’ve initiated! From your description, it seems that you’re not using any attractant/lure at the station. Most of the carnivore camera trap surveys I’ve conducted and been involved with have used such an attractant—I’ve now moved away from bait, and to a more standardized lure—to help bump up detection probability. It would have the same general effect of identifying the best trail or feature, but be more repeatable and easier to find deployment sites. I realize if microhabitat assessment is your intention, a lure can be an issue, but as you say, "all the species we study have home ranges far larger than a camera's field of view, so in a sense we are never really sampling "the site", only a tiny, imperfect window into it,” and a lure simply enlarges that window.

I’ll be curious to follow the discussion, and hear what you ultimately decide on!

Cheers,

Robert

